# M33 — audit_event's append-only trigger attached WHEN (false), so it never fires.
#
# WHY THE TRIGGER AND NOT THE GRANTS. `authenticated` holds SELECT on audit_event and
# nothing else, so a client is stopped by the ABSENT GRANT and never reaches a trigger at
# all. Grants do not bind the table OWNER, which is what migrations and definer functions
# run as — V32 recorded `dr_no_update` raising ST001 for the owner too, and that property
# is the only thing standing between a future migration and a quietly rewritten history.
#
# So this mutation is invisible to any test that only probes the client path: the 42501
# legs keep passing, because the grant is untouched. It is caught solely by the leg that
# probes as the owner, which is why that leg exists as a separate assertion rather than as
# a second expectation inside the client one.
#
# `WHEN (false)` rather than dropping the trigger: the trigger still exists, still names
# its function, and still appears in the catalogue, so a shape check counting triggers
# would not notice either. M14 uses the same technique on the publish-immutability trigger
# for the same reason.
id: M33
title: audit_event's append-only trigger attached WHEN (false), so UPDATE and DELETE by the owner are permitted
expect: caught
proves: audit history is append-only for the TABLE OWNER and not merely for clients, so a migration cannot rewrite it
expect-test: [FR-16] the app role is stopped by the ABSENT GRANT, and the owner by the TRIGGER

file: supabase/migrations/20261023080000_audit_event.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
create trigger audit_no_update_delete
  before update or delete on public.audit_event
  for each row execute function app.audit_no_change();
@@REPLACE
create trigger audit_no_update_delete
  before update or delete on public.audit_event
  for each row when (false) execute function app.audit_no_change();
@@END
